<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:7%"><span>7%</span></div></div></div><div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Include maternity</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:86%"><span>86%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:55%"><span>55%</span></div></div></div>
</figure><p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Decide which of these three your employees would use most, then price that one first. <a href="https://www.plumhq.com/get-quote">See where your current policy sits against companies like yours</a>.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><p>None of these is right for every company. Parental cover adds the most cost, so it is worth knowing how many of your employees would use it. A higher sum insured matters most where a single hospitalisation could exhaust ₹5 lakh. Maternity matters most for a workforce at family-building age. The useful question is which of these your employees would value enough to justify the cost.</p></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-3"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 3 of 5</div><p class="ch-title">How pricing works</p><p class="ch-desc">The inputs insurers price on, why the same company can land anywhere in the range, and what add-ons cost.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india">How GHI is priced</a></li><li><a href="#why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range">Why quotes vary</a></li><li><a href="#whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost">Base policy and add-ons</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h"><div class="section-label">Part 5 · How pricing works</div><h2 id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h">How group health insurance is priced in India</h2><p class="section-intro">Insurers price a group health policy using eight main inputs. Any one of these can move the premium by 10 to 50 percent, and the interaction between them is what produces the width of the ranges in this guide.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3 id="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured">Sum insured</h3><p>The headline cover amount per employee or family floater. Doubling the sum insured does not double the premium; the relationship is closer to a 40 to 60 percent uplift at each step, because the chance of a claim above ₹2 lakh is much lower than the chance of any claim at all. The table shows indicative PEPY ranges for an E+S+C policy at a mid-size company (51 to 500 employees) in a low-risk industry.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Sum insured per family</th>
<th>Indicative PEPY range</th>
<th>Typical use case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>₹1 lakh</td>
<td>₹2,500 to ₹5,000</td>
<td>Statutory minimum top-up, blue-collar workforce</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹2 lakh</td>
<td>₹3,500 to ₹7,000</td>
<td>Entry-level cover, often paired with ESIC</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹3 lakh</td>
<td>₹4,500 to ₹8,500</td>
<td>Common SMB and startup baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹5 lakh</td>
<td>₹6,500 to ₹12,000</td>
<td>Standard mid-market cover</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹7.5 lakh</td>
<td>₹9,000 to ₹16,000</td>
<td>Richer mid-market and tech companies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh</td>
<td>₹12,000 to ₹20,000</td>
<td>Senior-weighted workforces, leadership tiers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh+</td>
<td>₹15,000 to ₹30,000+</td>
<td>Leadership-only or super top-up structures</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><h3>Family definition</h3><p>The combination of dependents covered. The main configurations are:</p><ul>
<li><strong>E</strong>: employee only</li>
<li><strong>E+S</strong>: employee and spouse</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C</strong>: employee, spouse, and children</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C+P</strong>: employee, spouse, children, and parents (or parents-in-law)</li>
</ul><p>Moving from E to E+S+C typically adds 60 to 110 percent to premium. Adding parents can add another 40 to 120 percent on top of that, depending on parent age bands and whether the cover is floater or individual.</p><h3>Age and gender profile of the covered population</h3><p>A workforce with a median age of 28 will price materially lower than one with a median age of 40, and a workforce with higher female representation will price higher for policies that include maternity. Insurers model age-bracket distributions, not averages, so a bimodal workforce (many under-30s and a leadership layer in their 50s) prices differently from a flat profile at the same mean age.</p><h3 id="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost">Industry and occupational risk</h3><p>Office-based roles sit at the low end of the risk curve; insurers load 5 to 25 percent for industries with elevated accident or occupational-illness profiles.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Technology, SaaS and professional services</strong>: the baseline. The PEPY ranges on this page apply directly.</li>
<li><strong>BFSI</strong>: similar occupational risk, but an older median age in traditional banking shifts PEPY 10 to 20 percent higher.</li>
<li><strong>Logistics and field sales</strong>: 10 to 20 percent loading, often with personal accident cover as a mandatory layer.</li>
<li><strong>Manufacturing and industrial</strong>: 15 to 25 percent loading, driven by accident risk and back and joint claims.</li>
<li><strong>Healthcare and pharma</strong>: office-based pharma prices as tech; hospital staff price as manufacturing.</li>
</ul><h3>Claims history (from renewal year 2 onward)</h3><p>For the first-year policy, insurers price on employee demographics and policy design. From the second renewal, actual claims experience takes over as the dominant input. A company with a loss ratio above 85 percent in year 1 should expect a 20 to 45 percent renewal hike in year 2, independent of market movement. A loss ratio under 50 percent can hold renewal increases to 5 to 15 percent.</p><h3>Add-ons and riders</h3><p>Maternity benefit, parental cover, OPD, mental health, modern treatments, infertility cover, critical illness riders, and personal accident each have their own cost structure. The uplift each adds to base premium is in the add-ons table below.</p><h3>Geography and hospital network</h3><p>Metro-heavy workforces price higher than tier-2 or tier-3 heavy workforces because urban hospital tariffs are higher. The cashless hospital network available to a given policy depends on the insurer; Plum works with multiple insurers including ICICI Lombard, HDFC ERGO, Bajaj Allianz, Star Health, Niva Bupa, and Aditya Birla Health Insurance, each with distinct network footprints.</p><h3>Group size</h3><p>Larger groups get better rates because of risk pooling and broker leverage with insurers. Plum writes GHI policies starting at a minimum group size of 7 employees, which is lower than most market defaults.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range" class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h"><div class="section-label">Part 6 · What moves your quote</div><h2 id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h">Why the same company lands at different points in the range</h2><p class="section-intro">A single 150-person tech company in Bengaluru can get quoted anywhere from ₹5,500 to ₹13,000 PEPY for what sounds like the same policy. The illustration below shows the levers that produce that spread for the same company.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>PEPY</th>
<th>What's different</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Low end</td>
<td>~₹5,500</td>
<td>₹3 lakh SI, E only, no maternity, 25 percent co-pay, ₹5,000 room-rent cap, 2-year disease waiting period kept</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mid</td>
<td>~₹8,500</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity included, no co-pay, single private room, waiting periods waived</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High end</td>
<td>~₹13,000</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity + parental (one parent) + OPD, no co-pay, no sub-limits, modern treatments rider</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><p>The low-end policy is cheaper on the invoice, but every rupee shaved off the premium is paid by someone. For these design choices, the person paying is the employee, at the moment of claim:</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Design choice to cut premium</th>
<th>Premium saving</th>
<th>What the employee pays at claim time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Add 20% co-pay on all claims</td>
<td>10% to 15%</td>
<td>20% of every hospitalisation bill, cash at discharge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add ₹5,000 room-rent cap</td>
<td>5% to 10%</td>
<td>Proportional deduction on all associated bills if they pick a higher-category room</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Keep 2-year disease-waiting period</td>
<td>3% to 7%</td>
<td>Full out-of-pocket cost for listed conditions in first 2 years</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add 10% disease-wise sub-limits (cataract, hernia, etc.)</td>
<td>2% to 5%</td>
<td>Any cost above the sub-limit cap</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exclude maternity</td>
<td>10% to 25%</td>
<td>Full delivery cost (₹60,000 to ₹3 lakh) or shift to spouse's policy</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>A premium cut is a transfer, not a saving</h3><p>A premium cut via co-pay and sub-limits moves cost from the employer's P&amp;L to the employee's bank account, triggered at the worst possible moment for the employee.</p><h3>Year 3 is priced by your year 1 design choices</h3><p>Claims experience is the dominant input to year 2 and year 3 pricing, and it is shaped by year 1 design. A company that buys the cheapest possible first-year policy, with high co-pays and tight sub-limits, often sees worse claims outcomes (employees delay care, or claims hit caps and get partially denied) and still ends up with high renewal hikes because the loss ratio reads badly on paper.</p><p>A buyer picking between a ₹5,500 PEPY policy and a ₹7,500 PEPY policy for the same company should model the three-year total, not just year 1. The cheaper policy frequently ends up more expensive by year 3.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost" class="sec" aria-labelledby="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h"><div class="section-label">Part 7 · Coverage and add-ons</div><h2 id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h">What's included in a base policy and what add-ons cost</h2><p class="section-intro">A base GHI policy typically includes in-patient hospitalisation (minimum 24 hours), day-care procedures, pre-hospitalisation expenses (30 to 60 days), post-hospitalisation expenses (60 to 90 days), ambulance charges, and domiciliary hospitalisation under defined conditions. ICU, room rent, and surgeon fees are included within sub-limits that the policy design specifies. Add-ons sit on top; the table shows the typical uplift each adds to base premium.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Add-on</th>
<th>Uplift on base premium</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Maternity benefit</td>
<td>+10% to +25%</td>
<td>Varies with waiting period, cap amount, and infant cover duration</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Parental cover (floater, both parents)</td>
<td>+40% to +120%</td>
<td>Highest-impact add-on; see the next chapter</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>OPD cover</td>
<td>+8% to +18%</td>
<td>Wallet model usually cheaper than claim-reimbursement model</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mental health cover</td>
<td>+2% to +6%</td>
<td>Mandatory IRDAI inclusion for in-patient; OPD mental health is add-on</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Modern treatments rider</td>
<td>+1% to +3%</td>
<td>Robotic surgery, oral chemotherapy, stem-cell therapy, 10 to 12 named procedures</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Infertility cover</td>
<td>+3% to +8%</td>
<td>IVF and ART; typically capped and age-restricted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Personal accident cover</td>
<td>+2% to +5%</td>
<td>Separate benefit, not a loading on base</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Critical illness rider</td>
<td>+4% to +10%</td>
<td>Lump-sum benefit on diagnosis of listed conditions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Map each add-on to last year's employee utilisation data, if you have it, rather than picking from the menu by intuition.</p></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-4"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 4 of 5</div><p class="ch-title">Levers you control</p><p class="ch-desc">Parental cover, tiering, and how to set a budget that survives the policy year.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione">Parental cover and tiering</a></li><li><a href="#how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance">How to budget</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione" class="sec" aria-labelledby="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h"><div class="section-label">Part 8 · Parental cover and tiering</div><h2 id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line</h2><p class="section-intro">Parental cover is the single most impactful add-on on premium and the one that most scaling companies get wrong. Tiering is how most companies offer it, and richer cover generally, without paying for it across the board.</p><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Claim severity is 3 to 5 times higher for parents</h3><p>Parent-age populations (55 to 75) generate claims that are both more frequent and larger than employee or dependent-child claims. A parent cohort can account for 50 to 70 percent of a policy's total claim payout while representing 20 to 30 percent of covered lives.</p><h3>Renewal hikes concentrate here</h3><p>A policy with uncapped parental cover frequently sees year-2 renewal increases of 35 to 60 percent, almost entirely driven by the parent segment. Policies without parental cover see much more stable renewal pricing.</p><h3 id="tiering-as-a-cost-lever">Tiering: richer cover without paying for it across the board</h3><p>Treating "the GHI policy" as one monolithic plan is a common and expensive mistake. Companies that tier can hold total employer cost flat while materially improving perceived benefit:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Role-based tiers</strong>: different sum insured and family definition by level. Leadership typically gets ₹10 lakh to ₹25 lakh with full parental cover; individual contributors ₹3 lakh to ₹5 lakh with E+S+C. Total employer cost can be 10 to 20 percent lower than offering the leadership policy to everyone.</li>
<li><strong>Voluntary top-ups</strong>: a base policy for everyone at a sensible floor (say ₹3 lakh SI, E+S+C), with employee-paid top-ups for higher sum insured, parents or OPD. Participation typically runs 30 to 55 percent of eligible employees.</li>
<li><strong>Buy-ups and dependent buy-ups</strong>: the employer funds a base, and employees pay to add parents or further dependents at a defined contribution split. Common in startups moving from E-only to E+S+C.</li>
<li><strong>Parent-specific design</strong>: a lower sum insured for parents than for the employee's immediate family, age-banded premium sharing (a higher employer share for parents under 60), or capped out-patient and modern-treatment cover on the parent segment.</li>
</ul><p>The design choice signals values. A company that funds parental cover for everyone signals extended-family care; one that offers it as a voluntary buy-up signals personal choice. Neither is correct in the abstract; the right structure depends on workforce demographics and what employees would value enough to justify the cost.</p></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h"><div class="section-label">Part 9 · Budgeting</div><h2 id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h">How to budget for group health insurance</h2><p class="section-intro">Three budgeting approaches, in increasing order of sophistication:</p><ol class="steps"><li class="step"><h3>Rule of thumb: percentage of CTC</h3><p>Most Indian companies spend 0.8 to 2.5 percent of total CTC on GHI premium. Tech and professional services companies cluster at 1.2 to 1.8 percent. This method is quick but imprecise, because CTC varies with city and seniority mix in ways that do not perfectly track hospitalisation risk.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Peer-set benchmarking</h3><p>Market averages are the wrong benchmark for most companies. Build a peer set of five to ten companies you actually compete with for talent (the ones you lose candidates to or win them from) and target the median of that set, with explicit decisions about where to sit above or below. A policy 25 percent below your peers is a retention risk that will not show up in a market-wide benchmark.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Design-first budgeting</h3><p>Start from the benefit the company wants to offer (sum insured, family definition, add-ons) and work backward to the premium. This is the right method for companies that treat GHI as a strategic lever rather than an expense line, and it is the one that typically produces the best employee outcomes.</p></li></ol><div class="callout callout-coral"><span aria-hidden="true" class="callout-icon">⚑</span><div><strong>Budget note:</strong> A full budget has three line items: the premium, a 10 to 20 percent reserve for mid-year drift, and the HR time it takes to run the programme.</div></div><div class="detail-body deep-acc-body"><h3>Reserve 10 to 20 percent for mid-year drift</h3><p>The PEPY you sign for is the opening balance. Mid-year endorsements add 10 to 20 percent to the annual outlay for most growing companies:</p><ul>
<li><strong>New joiners</strong>: pro-rated against the annual premium, often at a slightly higher per-month rate than the base.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-term dependent additions</strong> (marriages, births, newly covered parents): typically the full annual premium for the dependent, not pro-rated.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-year sum insured upgrades</strong>: the full differential, with underwriting at the new tier.</li>
<li><strong>Add-ons activated mid-term</strong> (a new OPD wallet, extended maternity): the full differential plus any administration fee.</li>
</ul><p>A finance team that budgets at the quoted PEPY without this reserve typically ends the policy year over budget.</p><h3>Count the HR hours</h3><p>For a 100-person company, a well-run programme takes 3 to 6 HR hours a month in steady state and 15 to 25 during renewal and enrolment. A poorly run one can take 15 to 20 hours a month in steady state alone, mostly on coverage queries, endorsements, claim escalations and pre-authorisation chasing. At a loaded HR cost of ₹1,200 to ₹2,500 an hour, that gap is ₹2.4 lakh to ₹7.2 lakh a year, which belongs in any comparison between brokers.</p></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-5"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 5 of 5</div><p class="ch-title">Quick answers</p><p class="ch-desc">The questions buyers ask most, answered in a paragraph each.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">FAQs</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="frequently-asked-questions" class="sec" aria-labelledby="frequently-asked-questions-h"><div class="section-label">Quick answers</div><h2 id="frequently-asked-questions-h">Frequently asked questions</h2><p class="section-intro">The questions founders, HR and finance teams ask most about group health insurance cost.</p><div class="deep-accordion faq-acc"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">How much does group health insurance cost per employee in India?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Across 3,500+ companies insured through Plum, half spend roughly ₹6,000 to ₹24,000 per employee per year. Where a company lands depends on sum insured, family definition, age mix, industry, and add-ons. A baseline ₹3 lakh sum insured policy with E+S+C family definition for a mid-size tech company usually prices between ₹4,500 and ₹8,500 PEPY.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is group health insurance cheaper than individual health insurance?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Group health insurance in India is typically 30 to 60 percent cheaper per rupee of cover than an equivalent individual retail health insurance policy, because insurers price group policies on pooled risk and because group policies have lower acquisition costs. Group policies also usually waive waiting periods and cover pre-existing conditions from day one, which retail policies do not.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">What is the minimum group size for group health insurance in India?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The IRDAI defines a group as any legally formed association of 7 or more members. Plum writes GHI policies starting at a minimum group size of 7 employees. Some insurers set higher minimums (10, 15, or 20 employees); others match the regulatory floor.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Do group health insurance premiums increase every year?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Usually yes. Renewal pricing is driven by actual claims experience in the prior year, plus a market-wide medical inflation factor of 10 to 15 percent per year. A policy with a loss ratio below 60 percent might see a 5 to 15 percent renewal increase. A policy with a loss ratio above 85 percent can see renewal increases of 25 to 45 percent or more.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is GST applicable on group health insurance premiums?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. GST on group health insurance premium is currently charged at 18 percent, and the GST portion is in addition to the premium amount. For a ₹10 lakh annual premium, the total invoice is ₹11.8 lakh including GST.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is group health insurance tax deductible for the employer?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Premium paid by the employer for employee group health insurance is deductible as a business expense under the Income Tax Act. GST paid on the premium is available as input tax credit for companies registered under GST, subject to the applicable rules.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Can employees pay for group health insurance themselves?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes, through voluntary top-ups and dependent buy-ups structured as salary-deducted contributions. Employee-paid premium for self and family may qualify for Section 80D deduction under personal income tax, subject to the usual limits.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">What happens if an employee leaves the company mid-year?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The leaving employee's cover ends on the last working day (or on the policy exit date the employer defines). Some policies offer portability to an individual retail policy on exit, which the employee pays for. The employer typically does not receive a pro-rated refund.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">How is group health insurance different from ESIC?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>ESIC is a statutory scheme for employees earning up to ₹21,000 per month, funded by employer and employee contributions, with access to ESIC-affiliated hospitals and dispensaries. Group health insurance is a private policy with broader hospital network choice and typically richer benefits. Many companies run both: ESIC for the covered segment and private GHI for the rest, or private GHI on top of ESIC for all employees. A private top-up for ESIC-covered employees typically costs ₹1,500 to ₹4,000 per employee a year for ₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh of cover.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Can I include parents in group health insurance?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Parental cover is an add-on that most insurers support, structured as a floater or individual sum insured for one or both parents (or parents-in-law). Parental cover typically adds 40 to 120 percent to base premium depending on parent age bands and sum insured.</p></div></details></div><a class="report-card" href="https://www.plumhq.com/standard-of-employee-benefits">
